# Upgrading to Howdy 2.2

Howdy 2.2 adds background jobs, notifications, caching, translations, a
rich text editor and islands. Most of it is new packages and new modules,
which change nothing until you use them. A few changes do need edits in an
app written for 2.1, mostly so that Howdy's own words can be translated:
they are listed below, each with the code before and after.

## What's new

- [Background jobs](/docs/jobs/overview) with `howdy_jobs`: work queued in
  your database inside your own transactions, retried when it fails, and
  [cron schedules](/docs/jobs/schedules) that run once per time across
  every node.
- [Notifications](/docs/notifications/overview) with
  `howdy_notifications`: an inbox kept until read, pushed live to every
  open page, a notification bell, [email and digests](/docs/notifications/delivery)
  and [Web Push](/docs/notifications/web-push).
- [Flash messages](/docs/controllers/flash-messages) in core: a short note
  that survives one redirect, in a signed cookie.
- [Conditional requests](/docs/controllers/conditional-requests) in core,
  for `304 Not Modified`, `412` and `cache-control`, and
  [caching](/docs/cache/overview) with `howdy_cache`, in memory or the
  database, invalidated by tag.
- [Internationalisation](/docs/i18n/overview): `howdy/locale` chooses the
  locale a request wants, `howdy/problem` describes validation failures
  so they can be put into any language, and `howdy_i18n` turns
  [message catalogues](/docs/i18n/catalogues) into typed Gleam functions
  and [formats](/docs/i18n/formatting) numbers, money and dates. Howdy UI
  and Howdy Auth come translated.
- [File storage](/docs/storage/overview) with `howdy_storage`, and
  [file uploads](/docs/controllers/forms#File-uploads) in `howdy/form`,
  which now reads `multipart/form-data`.
- A [rich text editor](/docs/ui/rich-text) in Howdy UI, with mentions and
  attachments, stored as a JSON document.
- [Islands](/docs/ui/islands): parts of a server-rendered page that run in
  the browser as Lustre apps.
- [Health checks](/docs/guides/health-checks) at `/up`, answered before
  routing and middleware, and `database.health_check`.
- [Listening for changes](/docs/database/overview#Listening-for-changes)
  on PostgreSQL with `postgres.listen`.

## Updating

Point every Howdy dependency at the new release, and add any of the new
packages you want:

```toml
howdy = { git = "https://github.com/mikeyjones/howdy.git", ref = "v2.1.1" }
howdy_ui = { git = "https://github.com/mikeyjones/howdy.git", ref = "v2.1.1", path = "ui" }
howdy_jobs = { git = "https://github.com/mikeyjones/howdy.git", ref = "v2.1.1", path = "jobs" }
```

Howdy Auth has a new migration, which adds a `locale` column to its users
table. It runs with the rest when you call `migration.run` with
`auth.schema()`, as you already do. Each new package that keeps data has a
`schema()` of its own to add to the list: `jobs.schema()`,
`notifications.schema()` and, for the database store,
`cache_database.schema()`.

## Breaking changes

### Validation reports problems

A validation rule now fails with a `Problem` from `howdy/problem` rather
than an English sentence, so the words can be chosen in the reader's
language. See [validation messages](/docs/i18n/overview#Validation-messages).

A rule of your own gives a `Custom` problem, with a short code for clients
and the English message:

```gleam
// 2.1
validate.custom(fn(name) { !string.contains(name, " ") }, "must not contain spaces")

// 2.2
validate.custom(
  fn(name) { !string.contains(name, " ") },
  problem.Custom("spaces", "must not contain spaces"),
)
```

The same goes for a rule written as a function, which now returns
`Error(problem)`, and for rules given to `schema.rule` in Howdy OpenAPI.

`service.FieldError` holds the problem in place of the message, and
`form.error` gives the problem. Put it into words with `problem.message`:

```gleam
// 2.1
case form.error(errors, "email") {
  Some(message) -> html.p([], [html.text(message)])
  None -> element.none()
}

// 2.2
case form.error(errors, "email") {
  Some(found) -> html.p([], [html.text(problem.message(found, problem.english()))])
  None -> element.none()
}
```

Use `problem.translated(locale)` in place of `problem.english()` for
another language. In tests, compare with the problem:
`FieldError("age", problem.TooSmall(13))`.

The JSON body of a `422` keeps each field's English `message`, and adds a
`code` and the details the code needs, such as `minimum`.

### Errors carry codes

`service.Error` has a new kind, `Coded(code, error)`, for an error with a
stable code that a client can translate. Every JSON error body now has a
`code`: the `Coded` error's own, or one for the kind, such as `not_found`.

A `case` over every kind of error needs a branch for `Coded`. Howdy Auth
now returns its errors coded, such as
`Coded("password_common", Invalid(...))`, so match on the kind inside with
`service.uncoded`:

```gleam
// 2.1
case auth.register_password(identity, email, password) {
  Error(service.Invalid(message)) -> show_form(message)
  ...
}

// 2.2
case
  auth.register_password(identity, email, password)
  |> result.map_error(service.uncoded)
{
  Error(service.Invalid(message)) -> show_form(message)
  ...
}
```

Howdy OpenAPI documents `code` as a required field of its error schema, so
clients generated from the document change too.

### Codecs moved to core

The codec `howdy_remote` used is now `howdy/codec`, in core, so that
`howdy_cache` can share it. `remote.Codec` is an alias for it, and
`remote.int()`, `remote.list(of:)` and the rest still work, but
`remote.Codec` can no longer be built or matched as a record:

```gleam
// 2.1
remote.Codec(encode: user_to_json, decoder: user_decoder())

// 2.2
codec.new(encode: user_to_json, decoder: user_decoder())
```

Remote procedures now send errors with their problems and codes. A node
on 2.2 cannot read a field error from a node on 2.1, and a node on 2.1
cannot read a coded error, so upgrade every node of a cluster together.

### Howdy UI components take words

Every string a component shows is now in one `howdy/ui/words.Words`
record, so it can be translated. Components that are plain functions take
the words as their first argument; for English, pass `words.english()`:

```gleam
// 2.1
ui.pagination(current: 3, total: 12, href: page_href)
toast.region([], toasts)

// 2.2
let w = words.english()
ui.pagination(w, current: 3, total: 12, href: page_href)
toast.region(w, [], toasts)
```

These take the words first:

- `breadcrumb.breadcrumb` and `ui.breadcrumb`
- `carousel.carousel`, `carousel.styled` and `ui.carousel`
- `chat.conversation` and `ui.chat_conversation`
- `command.dialog`, `command.combobox`, `command.multiple_combobox`,
  `ui.command_dialog`, `ui.combobox` and `ui.multiple_combobox`
- `drawer.drawer` and `ui.drawer`
- `pagination.pagination`, `pagination.live_pagination` and
  `ui.pagination`
- `questionnaire.view`
- `slider.range` and `slider.thumbs`
- `toast.region`, `toast.view` and `ui.toast_region`

`attachment.attachment`, `attachment.styled` and `ui.attachment` take
`words:` too, but the attachment's own, `attachment.english()`, or the
`attachment` field of `Words`.

Components built step by step, such as `data_table` and `chart`, have a
`words` step instead, and speak English without one.

`chat.conversation_with`, which changed the jump button's label, is gone:
give `conversation` words with another `jump_to_newest`, as in
`Words(..words.english(), jump_to_newest: "Newest")`.

### Calendar locales

`calendar.Locale` now writes the heading and the day numbers itself, and
knows the day weeks start on, so that a locale from CLDR fits.
`month_names` is gone; `heading` writes a month and year instead, and
there are `weekday_abbreviations`, `day_number` and `first_day`. The
easiest way to a whole locale is `words.translated(t).calendar`. For one
written by hand, start from English:

```gleam
// 2.1
calendar.Locale(..calendar.english(), month_names: ["janvier", ...])

// 2.2
calendar.Locale(..calendar.english(), heading: fn(year, month) {
  month_name(month) <> " " <> int.to_string(year)
})
```

`calendar.locale` now also starts weeks on the locale's first day.
`calendar.sunday_first`, or the new `calendar.first_day`, overrides it.

### Howdy Auth users have a locale

`user.User` has a new field, `locale`, the language a user's pages and
emails use. Code that builds a `User`, such as a test, or matches one by
position, needs it.

`emails.with_mfa_template` gives its template the recipient's locale as
a third argument, an `Option(String)`, and `emails.mfa_message` and
`emails.default_mfa` take it as their last:

```gleam
// 2.1
emails.with_mfa_template(emails, fn(email, code) { my_mfa_email(email, code) })

// 2.2
emails.with_mfa_template(emails, fn(email, code, locale) {
  my_mfa_email(email, code, locale)
})
```

## Package versions

Howdy 2.2.0 is made of:

- `howdy` 2.2.0
- `howdy_admin` 2.1.0
- `howdy_auth` 2.1.0
- `howdy_cache` 2.0.0, new
- `howdy_database` 2.1.0
- `howdy_dev` 2.1.0
- `howdy_flags` 2.0.0
- `howdy_i18n` 2.0.0, new
- `howdy_jobs` 2.0.0, new
- `howdy_mail` 2.1.0
- `howdy_notifications` 2.0.0, new
- `howdy_openapi` 2.1.0
- `howdy_remote` 2.1.0
- `howdy_storage` 2.0.0, new
- `howdy_telemetry` 2.0.0
- `howdy_ui` 2.1.0
